        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: When was the David Jones House built?\n\nAnswer A: 1912\n  about: David H. Jones House\n  sense: inception\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 2\n  evidence: \"The house was built in 1912 for David H. and Mary E. Nielsen Jones,\" [document 1]; \"inception 1912 1 reference imported from Wikimedia project English Wikipedia Wikimedia import URL https : //en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php ? title=David_H._Jones_House & oldid=916272711\" [document 2]\n\nAnswer B: 1895\n  about: David H. Jones House\n  sense: inception (the document does not state it)\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"[The house] was built in 1895 for David H. and Mary E. Nielsen Jones,\" [document 3]\n\nAnswer C: 1874\n  about: David H. Jones House\n  sense: inception (the document does not state it)\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"The David Jones House on High St. in Maryville, Tennessee, also known as the Elmer B. Waller House, was built in 1874 by Maryville builder David Jones.\" [document 4]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: David H. Jones House The David H. Jones House is located at 143 South Main Street in Spanish Fork, Utah, United States and was added to the National Register of Historic Places in 1985. The house was built in 1912 for David H. and Mary E. Nielsen Jones, who lived there until their deaths in 1959 and 1976, respectively. In addition to running his own farm and livestock operations, David Jones served as Commissioner of Agriculture for Utah, as president of the Utah State Farm Bureau, and as president of the Utah County Cooperative Dairy for 20 years. His political\nDocument 2: Related changes Permanent link Page information Concept URI Cite this page Get shortened URL Download QR code Print/export Create a book Download as PDF Printable version In other projects Appearance move to sidebar hide From Wikidata Spanish Fork , Utah , NRHP-listed in Utah County edit Language Label Description Also known as default for all languages No label defined – English David H. Jones House Spanish Fork , Utah , NRHP-listed in Utah County Statements instance of house 0 references inception 1912 1 reference imported from Wikimedia project English Wikipedia Wikimedia import URL https : //en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php ? title=David_H._Jones_House & oldid=916272711\nDocument 3: [Close up view of the David H. Jones House, June 2018] The house was built in 1895 for David H. and Mary E. Nielsen Jones, who lived there until their deaths in 1959 and 1976, respectively. In addition to running his own farm and livestock operations, David Jones served as the Commissioner of Agriculture for Utah, the president of the Utah State Farm Bureau, and as the president of the Utah County Cooperative Dairy for 20 years. His political career included six years of service as a Spanish Fork city councilman.\nDocument 4: David Jones House (High Street, Maryville, Tennessee) The David Jones House on High St. in Maryville, Tennessee, also known as the Elmer B. Waller House, was built in 1874 by Maryville builder David Jones. Jones ran a brick company and built numerous brick buildings in Maryville including the Blount County Courthouse (which burned in 1906). The house is one of two best examples of Italianate architecture, along with the Peter Bartlett House, in Blount County. It was listed on the National Register of Historic Places (NRHP) in 1989. David Jones also built the county's only Second Empire style house on\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write a double quote as \\\"."
